Lab 18: Integrating RHACM with Ansible
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Understand the integration architecture between Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) and Ansible
Configure Ansible collections and inventory to interact with RHACM APIs on a hub cluster
Build Ansible roles and playbooks that query RHACM status and manage ManagedCluster resources
Automate RHACM Application/Subscription deployment across managed clusters using Ansible
Validate automation workflows and troubleshoot common integration failures using real error messages
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes concepts (namespaces, custom resources, labels/selectors)
Familiarity with Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) fundamentals (hub/managed cluster model, ManagedCluster, Application/Subscription resources)
Working knowledge of Ansible playbooks, roles, and variables
Experience with YAML syntax
Basic Linux command-line skills
This lab assumes access to an isolated practice environment (a lab-provided cloud machine or your own sandbox cluster) with an RHACM hub already installed, or a plain Kubernetes/OpenShift cluster where you can simulate the RHACM custom resources for automation practice. All commands below are run against your own environment for defensive, hands-on learning purposes only.

Install the required tooling before starting:

# Verify Python (3.8+) and pip
python3 --version
pip3 --version

# Install Ansible core if not already present
pip3 install --user ansible-core

# Verify Ansible installation
ansible --version

# Install the Kubernetes collection (this is the correct, real collection
# for interacting with Kubernetes/OpenShift/RHACM custom resources -
# there is no separate "redhat.rhacm" collection; RHACM resources are
# managed as standard Kubernetes custom resources via kubernetes.core)
ansible-galaxy collection install kubernetes.core

# Verify the collection installed correctly
ansible-galaxy collection list kubernetes.core

# The kubernetes.core collection also requires the Python kubernetes client
pip3 install --user kubernetes
Lab Environment Setup
You need access to a Kubernetes or OpenShift cluster with kubectl (or oc) configured via a valid kubeconfig file. If your cluster has RHACM installed, you will interact with the real open-cluster-management namespace and its custom resources. If you do not have RHACM installed, you can still complete every task: the playbooks below create/read standard Kubernetes custom resources whose API groups match RHACM's real API groups, so the same automation logic applies once RHACM is installed in a production-like environment.

Confirm cluster access before proceeding:

kubectl config current-context
kubectl get nodes
Expected result: kubectl get nodes returns at least one node in Ready status. If this fails, fix cluster access before continuing â�� none of the later tasks will work without it.

Task 1: Configure Ansible to Query and Manage RHACM Resources
Subtask 1.1: Set Up the Ansible Project Structure
Create the project directory and layout:
mkdir -p ~/rhacm-ansible-lab/{playbooks,inventory,roles}
cd ~/rhacm-ansible-lab
Create the Ansible configuration file:
cat > ansible.cfg << 'EOF'
[defaults]
inventory = inventory/hosts.yml
host_key_checking = False
stdout_callback = yaml
gathering = explicit
retry_files_enabled = False
roles_path = roles
EOF
Create the inventory file. Replace /home/student/.kube/config with the actual path to your kubeconfig if different (check with echo $KUBECONFIG or use the default ~/.kube/config):
cat > inventory/hosts.yml << 'EOF'
all:
  children:
    rhacm_hubs:
      hosts:
        hub-cluster:
          ansible_connection: local
          kubeconfig_path: "{{ lookup('env', 'HOME') }}/.kube/config"
          rhacm_namespace: open-cluster-management
EOF
Verify the inventory parses correctly:
ansible-inventory -i inventory/hosts.yml --list
Deliverable: The ansible-inventory command outputs valid JSON containing a hub-cluster host under rhacm_hubs with the kubeconfig_path variable set to your resolved home directory path.

Subtask 1.2: Create a Role to Report RHACM Hub Status
Scaffold the role:
ansible-galaxy init roles/rhacm_status
Write the tasks file for the role:
cat > roles/rhacm_status/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Check whether the RHACM namespace exists
  kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
    api_version: v1
    kind: Namespace
    name: "{{ rhacm_namespace | default('open-cluster-management') }}"
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
  register: rhacm_ns_info

- name: Report RHACM namespace presence
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    msg: >-
      RHACM namespace '{{ rhacm_namespace | default('open-cluster-management') }}' is
      {{ 'present' if rhacm_ns_info.resources else 'NOT FOUND - RHACM may not be installed' }}

- name: Query MultiClusterHub status (only if namespace exists)
  kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
    api_version: operator.open-cluster-management.io/v1
    kind: MultiClusterHub
    namespace: "{{ rhacm_namespace | default('open-cluster-management') }}"
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
  register: mch_status
  when: rhacm_ns_info.resources | length > 0
  ignore_errors: true

- name: Report MultiClusterHub status
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    msg: >-
      {{ 'MultiClusterHub phase: ' + mch_status.resources[0].status.phase
      if (mch_status is defined and mch_status.resources is defined and mch_status.resources | length > 0)
      else 'MultiClusterHub resource not found (RHACM operator not installed or CRD missing)' }}

- name: List existing ManagedCluster resources
  kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
    api_version: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1
    kind: ManagedCluster
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
  register: managed_clusters
  ignore_errors: true

- name: Report managed cluster count
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    msg: >-
      {{ 'Found ' + (managed_clusters.resources | length | string) + ' managed cluster(s)'
      if (managed_clusters is defined and managed_clusters.resources is defined)
      else 'ManagedCluster CRD not found - RHACM cluster management API is not available' }}
EOF
Create a playbook that runs the role:
cat > playbooks/check_rhacm_status.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Report RHACM Hub Status via Ansible
  hosts: rhacm_hubs
  gather_facts: false
  tasks:
    - name: Include rhacm_status role
      ansible.builtin.include_role:
        name: rhacm_status
EOF
Run it:
ansible-playbook playbooks/check_rhacm_status.yml
Deliverable: Console output shows three debug messages: RHACM namespace presence, MultiClusterHub phase (or a clear "not found" message), and a managed cluster count (or a clear CRD-not-found message). If RHACM is installed, you should see phase: Running and at least Found 0 managed cluster(s) (the hub itself is not counted as a ManagedCluster unless self-managed).

Task 2: Automate ManagedCluster Registration and Application Deployment
Subtask 2.1: Create a Role to Manage ManagedCluster Resources
Scaffold the role:
ansible-galaxy init roles/cluster_management
Write the tasks:
cat > roles/cluster_management/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Create namespace for the managed cluster
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: Namespace
      metadata:
        name: "{{ cluster_name }}"

- name: Create ManagedCluster resource
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1
      kind: ManagedCluster
      metadata:
        name: "{{ cluster_name }}"
        labels:
          cloud: "{{ cloud_provider | default('auto-detect') }}"
          environment: "{{ cluster_environment | default('development') }}"
      spec:
        hubAcceptsClient: true
  register: mc_result

- name: Report ManagedCluster creation result
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    msg: "ManagedCluster '{{ cluster_name }}' changed: {{ mc_result.changed }}"
EOF
Create a playbook that registers a test ManagedCluster entry:
cat > playbooks/register_cluster.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Register a ManagedCluster via Ansible
  hosts: rhacm_hubs
  gather_facts: false
  vars:
    cluster_name: "test-cluster-ansible"
    cloud_provider: "aws"
    cluster_environment: "testing"
  tasks:
    - name: Include cluster_management role
      ansible.builtin.include_role:
        name: cluster_management
EOF
Run the playbook and verify:
ansible-playbook playbooks/register_cluster.yml

kubectl get managedcluster test-cluster-ansible
kubectl get namespace test-cluster-ansible
Deliverable: kubectl get managedcluster test-cluster-ansible returns the resource with hubAcceptsClient: true visible via kubectl get managedcluster test-cluster-ansible -o yaml, and the matching namespace exists. Note: this registers the ManagedCluster object itself; a real managed cluster still needs its klusterlet agent to establish the actual connection, which is outside the scope of this automation exercise.

Subtask 2.2: Automate Application Deployment with a Subscription
Scaffold the role:
ansible-galaxy init roles/application_deployment
Write the tasks:
cat > roles/application_deployment/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Create application namespace
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: Namespace
      metadata:
        name: "{{ app_namespace }}"

- name: Create Channel pointing to the Git repository
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
      kind: Channel
      metadata:
        name: "{{ app_name }}-channel"
        namespace: "{{ app_namespace }}"
      spec:
        type: Git
        pathname: "{{ git_repo_url }}"

- name: Create PlacementRule for target clusters
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
      kind: PlacementRule
      metadata:
        name: "{{ app_name }}-placement"
        namespace: "{{ app_namespace }}"
        labels:
          app: "{{ app_name }}"
      spec:
        clusterConditions:
          - status: "True"
            type: ManagedClusterConditionAvailable
        clusterSelector:
          matchLabels: "{{ cluster_selector | default({}) }}"

- name: Create Subscription linking channel to placement
  kubernetes.core.k8s:
    state: present
    kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
    definition:
      apiVersion: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
      kind: Subscription
      metadata:
        name: "{{ app_name }}-subscription"
        namespace: "{{ app_namespace }}"
        labels:
          app: "{{ app_name }}"
      spec:
        channel: "{{ app_namespace }}/{{ app_name }}-channel"
        placement:
          placementRef:
            name: "{{ app_name }}-placement"
            kind: PlacementRule
  register: sub_result

- name: Report subscription creation result
  ansible.builtin.debug:
    msg: "Subscription '{{ app_name }}-subscription' changed: {{ sub_result.changed }}"
EOF
Create a playbook that deploys a sample application:
cat > playbooks/deploy_application.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy an Application via RHACM Subscription
  hosts: rhacm_hubs
  gather_facts: false
  vars:
    app_name: "nginx-demo"
    app_namespace: "rhacm-applications"
    git_repo_url: "https://github.com/open-cluster-management-io/multicloud-operators-subscription"
    cluster_selector:
      environment: development
  tasks:
    - name: Include application_deployment role
      ansible.builtin.include_role:
        name: application_deployment
EOF
Run it and verify:
ansible-playbook playbooks/deploy_application.yml

kubectl get channel -n rhacm-applications
kubectl get placementrule -n rhacm-applications
kubectl get subscription -n rhacm-applications
Deliverable: All three commands return the nginx-demo-channel, nginx-demo-placement, and nginx-demo-subscription resources in the rhacm-applications namespace. Running kubectl describe subscription nginx-demo-subscription -n rhacm-applications shows the Channel field correctly pointing to rhacm-applications/nginx-demo-channel.

Subtask 2.3: Build a Combined Validation Playbook
Create a validation playbook that checks every resource created so far and writes a report:
cat > playbooks/validate_integration.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Validate RHACM-Ansible Integration
  hosts: rhacm_hubs
  gather_facts: true
  tasks:
    - name: Check ManagedCluster test-cluster-ansible
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1
        kind: ManagedCluster
        name: test-cluster-ansible
        kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
      register: mc_check
      ignore_errors: true

    - name: Check Subscription nginx-demo-subscription
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: apps.open-cluster-management.io/v1
        kind: Subscription
        name: nginx-demo-subscription
        namespace: rhacm-applications
        kubeconfig: "{{ kubeconfig_path }}"
      register: sub_check
      ignore_errors: true

    - name: Build validation results
      ansible.builtin.set_fact:
        validation_results:
          - component: "ManagedCluster"
            found: "{{ (mc_check.resources is defined) and (mc_check.resources | length > 0) }}"
          - component: "Subscription"
            found: "{{ (sub_check.resources is defined) and (sub_check.resources | length > 0) }}"

    - name: Display validation summary
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "{{ item.component }}: {{ 'FOUND' if item.found else 'MISSING' }}"
      loop: "{{ validation_results }}"

    - name: Write validation report to a local file
      ansible.builtin.copy:
        content: |
          RHACM-Ansible Integration Validation Report
          Generated: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
          {% for item in validation_results %}
          {{ item.component }}: {{ 'FOUND' if item.found else 'MISSING' }}
          {% endfor %}
        dest: /tmp/rhacm-ansible-validation-report.txt
      delegate_to: localhost
EOF
Run the validation and inspect the report:
ansible-playbook playbooks/validate_integration.yml

cat /tmp/rhacm-ansible-validation-report.txt
Deliverable: /tmp/rhacm-ansible-validation-report.txt exists and lists ManagedCluster: FOUND and Subscription: FOUND (assuming Subtasks 2.1 and 2.2 completed successfully), along with a timestamp from ansible_date_time.iso8601.

Subtask 2.4 (Optional): Prepare for Monitoring Integration
If your cluster has the Prometheus Operator installed, RHACM metrics can be exposed via ServiceMonitor and PrometheusRule custom resources. Before creating these resources, always verify the Prometheus Operator CRDs exist â�� otherwise the resources will be created successfully but silently have no effect (no controller will reconcile them):

kubectl get crd servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com
Expected result: this returns a CRD named servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com. If it returns Error from server (NotFound), the Prometheus Operator is not installed, and you should skip creating ServiceMonitor/PrometheusRule resources until it is â�� creating them anyway will not raise an error but will not produce any working monitoring either. Installing the Prometheus Operator is outside the scope of this lab; treat this step as a readiness check to apply in your own environment.

Expected Outcomes
A working Ansible project (~/rhacm-ansible-lab) with roles and playbooks that query RHACM hub status, register a ManagedCluster, and deploy an Application/Subscription entirely through kubernetes.core modules
A validation playbook and generated report confirming that Ansible-managed RHACM resources exist on the cluster
Practical understanding of which RHACM API groups (cluster.open-cluster-management.io, apps.open-cluster-management.io, operator.open-cluster-management.io) Ansible must target, and how to detect missing prerequisites (RHACM operator, Prometheus Operator) before assuming automation failures
Troubleshooting
ERROR! couldn't resolve module/action 'kubernetes.core.k8s'

Cause: the kubernetes.core collection is not installed for the Ansible interpreter being used, or it was installed for a different user/virtualenv.
Diagnostic hint: run ansible-galaxy collection list kubernetes.core and pip3 show kubernetes to confirm both the collection and its required Python library are present for the same Python environment ansible-playbook is using.
Error: "redhat.rhacm" not found

Cause: this collection name does not exist on Ansible Galaxy. RHACM resources are plain Kubernetes custom resources, not a separate vendor collection.
Solution: use kubernetes.core.k8s and kubernetes.core.k8s_info against the correct RHACM API groups (e.g., cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1), as done throughout this lab.
Failed to find exact match for operator.open-cluster-management.io/v1.MultiClusterHub by [kind, name]

Cause: the RHACM operator is not installed, so the MultiClusterHub CRD does not exist on this cluster.
Diagnostic hint: run kubectl get crd multiclusterhubs.operator.open-cluster-management.io. If it returns NotFound, install RHACM first or treat this environment as a non-RHACM sandbox and focus on the kubernetes.core module mechanics instead.
Namespace "test-cluster-ansible" is forbidden: ... cannot create resource "namespaces"

Cause: the kubeconfig's current user lacks RBAC permission to create namespaces or cluster-scoped resources.
Diagnostic hint: run kubectl auth can-i create namespaces and kubectl auth can-i create managedclusters.cluster.open-cluster-management.io to confirm your permissions before re-running the playbook.
Conclusion
In this lab, students built a working Ansible automation layer around RHACM by installing and using the kubernetes.core collection, writing roles that query hub status, register ManagedCluster resources, and deploy applications through Channel, PlacementRule, and Subscription custom resources. Along the way, students learned to verify prerequisites explicitly â�� checking for the MultiClusterHub CRD, the ManagedCluster CRD, and the Prometheus Operator CRDs â�� rather than assuming a failed lookup means broken automation. The validation playbook tied these pieces together into a single repeatable check with a generated report, mirroring how real GitOps and cluster-fleet automation pipelines confirm state after applying changes. From here, students can extend this project by adding real managed clusters with klusterlet agents, wiring the Subscription's PlacementRule to live cluster labels, or integrating the validation playbook into a CI pipeline that runs after every automation change.
